 agents=[{'id':'nivolumab','name':'Nivolumab','alias':'Opdivo','type':'Immunotherapy','mechanism':'Blocks the PD-1 immune checkpoint','explanation':'Nivolumab binds PD-1 on T cells. It prevents signals that can reduce the immune attack on cancer. This can also let immune cells inflame healthy tissues. It is infused every 21 days in this plan.','visual':['T cell','PD-1 blocked','Immune response'],'watch':'New breathing, bowel, skin, hormone or other symptoms need prompt advice during or after treatment.','source_ids':['nivo','nccp'],'risks':['Immune inflammation in multiple organs','Infusion reactions','Delayed effects after treatment stops'],'symptoms':['Breathing / cough','Diarrhoea','Fatigue']},{'id':'ipilimumab','name':'Ipilimumab','alias':'Yervoy','type':'Immunotherapy','mechanism':'Blocks the CTLA-4 immune checkpoint','explanation':'Ipilimumab blocks CTLA-4, another control on T-cell activity. It works alongside nivolumab through a different immune checkpoint. It is given every 42 days, so it is absent from each Day 22 visit. Healthy organs can also become inflamed.','visual':['T cell','CTLA-4 blocked','Immune activity'],'watch':'Report diarrhoea, tummy pain, rash, severe tiredness, headache or other new symptoms promptly. Keep your immune alert card.','source_ids':['ipi','nccp'],'risks':['Immune bowel, liver, skin and hormone inflammation','Other serious organ inflammation','Infusion reactions'],'symptoms':['Diarrhoea','Skin / nail changes','Fatigue']},{'id':'pemetrexed','name':'Pemetrexed','alias':'Alimta','type':'Chemotherapy','mechanism':'Disrupts DNA/RNA building','explanation':'Pemetrexed blocks enzymes needed to make DNA and RNA building blocks. Dividing cancer cells are affected, but healthy blood-forming and mouth-lining cells can also be injured. Prescribed folic acid and B12 reduce toxicity; do not omit them because pemetrexed is an antifolate. There are two planned doses in this regimen.','visual':['DNA building blocks','Supply disrupted','Cell division slows'],'watch':'Low blood counts, sore mouth, rash, sickness and kidney problems can occur.','source_ids':['pem','nccp'],'risks':['Low blood counts and infection','Mouth soreness, rash and fatigue','Kidney toxicity'],'symptoms':['Mouth soreness / ulcers','Fatigue','Skin / nail changes']},{'id':plat,'name':Plat,'alias':'Platinum chemotherapy','type':'Chemotherapy','mechanism':'Damages DNA','explanation':Plat+' forms links in DNA that hinder copying and repair. Healthy tissues can also be harmed. '+('Its dose depends on target exposure (AUC) and measured or estimated kidney function; AUC is not a fixed number of milligrams.' if carbo else 'Kidney injury, changes in salts in the blood, hearing damage and nerve symptoms are important risks. Extra fluids and electrolyte checks are part of treatment.')+' There are two planned doses in this regimen.','visual':['DNA','Platinum links','Copying disrupted'],'watch':('Report fever, bleeding, nausea, hearing changes or numbness. Tell the nurse immediately about possible allergy during the infusion.' if carbo else 'Report reduced urine, vomiting, ringing in the ears, hearing change or numbness. Hydration instructions are individual.'),'source_ids':['nccp'],'risks':['Low blood counts and infection','Nausea and vomiting','Kidney, hearing and nerve effects','Allergic reactions'],'symptoms':['Numbness / tingling','Bleeding / bruising','Fatigue']}]
 chapters=[{'id':'understanding','title':'Why this combination is offered','intro':purpose,'items':[item('What do the cancer terms mean?','Metastatic means cancer has spread beyond where it started. NSCLC is a broad lung-cancer group; non-squamous describes one histology, often adenocarcinoma. First-line means the first systemic treatment for this setting. The pathology and molecular results help determine whether this exact pemetrexed combination suits your cancer.'),item('What does the evidence mean for me?','In CheckMate 9LA, the strategy of two immune medicines with a short chemotherapy course helped people live longer than chemotherapy alone. The study included different histologies and platinum medicines. It cannot tell exactly what benefit you will have, or prove that this particular platinum is better for you.',['trial']),item('What are the alternatives?','Ask how tumour tests, fitness and your priorities affect the options, including other drug combinations, targeted treatment when relevant, a clinical trial or symptom-focused care. Support for breathlessness, pain, appetite and emotional concerns can accompany anticancer treatment.')]},{'id':'preparation','title':'Before the first visit','intro':'The preventive medicines and baseline review are part of the treatment.','items':[item('Describe your usual health','Tell the team about your usual breathing and bowel pattern, autoimmune illness, lung inflammation, infections, nerve or hearing symptoms, diabetes, and all prescription or non-prescription medicines. This helps identify a new problem promptly.'),item('Folate, B12 and steroid plan',vitamins+' '+steroids),item('Prepare contact and practical support','Keep your oncology and out-of-hours instructions, a thermometer, treatment names and immune alert card available. Arrange blood tests, transport and help at home where needed. Ask what to do if a support medicine is missed; do not double a dose without advice.')]},{'id':'schedule','title':'Two chemotherapy visits, then continuing immunotherapy','intro':schedule,'items':[item('Day 1 and Day 22 differ','Cycle 1 Day 1 includes all four medicines. Day 22 is three weeks later and includes nivolumab plus the second and final planned chemotherapy doses, but no ipilimumab. A 42-day cycle is six weeks.'),item('The next 42-day cycles','From Cycle 2, Day 1 includes nivolumab and ipilimumab. Day 22 includes nivolumab only. No pemetrexed or platinum is planned in this continuation phase. The support medicines change too; check the updated prescription.'),item('How long does treatment last?',duration+' The team reviews benefit, symptoms and scans. A delay can allow investigation or recovery. It does not mean you have failed treatment.') ]},{'id':'monitoring','title':'What the checks are looking for','intro':'Different checks assess safety today, possible toxicity and cancer response.','items':[item('Blood count, kidney and liver','Blood counts and kidney/liver tests are checked before each Day 1 and Day 22 treatment. Blood counts show infection-fighting cells, oxygen-carrying cells and platelets. Kidney results influence chemotherapy safety. Normal results from a previous visit do not rule out a new problem.'),item('Hormones and immune effects','Glucose and thyroid tests are checked before each 42-day cycle. The baseline assessment includes morning cortisol and hepatitis tests. New symptoms can require extra tests between planned visits; routine blood tests alone cannot exclude all immune side effects.'),item('Scans and decisions','Your consultant sets the scan schedule. The team considers symptoms, examination, results and what matters to you. Side effects are not a reliable measure of how well treatment is controlling cancer.')]},{'id':'chemotherapy-effects','title':'Effects around the chemotherapy visits','intro':'These are selected possibilities, not a forecast or a complete medicine leaflet.','items':[item('Sickness, appetite and constipation','Take preventive anti-sickness medicines as prescribed. Tell the team if they do not control symptoms. Persistent vomiting, difficulty keeping fluids down or much less urine need prompt advice. Some anti-sickness and pain medicines cause constipation; ask for a bowel plan.'),item('Blood counts and infection','Fever, shivers or suddenly feeling unwell need immediate oncology advice, even with a normal temperature. Counts can fall between visits and cannot be judged from the calendar. Report unexpected bruising, bleeding or worsening breathlessness.'),item('Mouth, skin and tiredness','Use gentle mouth care and report ulcers, painful swallowing, rash or reduced intake. Balance rest with manageable activity. Marked or sudden weakness needs assessment: fatigue can also be caused by infection, anaemia or immune hormone problems.'),item('Kidneys, hearing and nerves',('Carboplatin dosing depends on kidney function. ' if carbo else 'Cisplatin can injure kidneys and affect blood salts, hearing and nerves. Fluids before and after the infusion reduce particular risks, and your fluid advice is individual. ')+'Tell the team about reduced urine, new ringing in the ears, hearing loss, tingling or numbness before another chemotherapy dose.')]},{'id':'immune-effects','title':'Immune effects need early reporting','intro':immune,'items':[item('Breathing, bowel, skin and urine','New cough or breathlessness, diarrhoea, blood or mucus in stool, severe abdominal pain, yellow eyes, dark urine, reduced urine or swelling need prompt assessment. Do not assume these are ordinary chemotherapy effects. Blistering or peeling skin or sores in the eyes or mouth need urgent care.'),item('Hormones, heart, muscles and nerves','Report unusual weakness, persistent headache, dizziness, marked thirst, frequent urination, palpitations, muscle weakness or vision changes. Severe chest pain, collapse, confusion or major breathing difficulty needs emergency care. Some hormone effects require long-term replacement.'),item('Treatment may need to pause','The team may withhold both immune medicines, investigate and prescribe steroids or other treatment. This is different from the short preventive steroid course around chemotherapy. Do not start leftover steroids, change their dose or abruptly stop a prescribed course.')]},{'id':'daily-life','title':'Living with treatment','intro':'The aim is to fit care around the things that matter to you.','items':[item('Food, fluids, work and movement','Ask for help with weight loss, reduced appetite, work adjustments, transport or household tasks. A fixed fluid target is not suitable for everyone. Do not drive if dizzy, very tired or affected by sedating medicines; cisplatin anti-sickness treatment can include olanzapine.'),item('Fertility, pregnancy and breastfeeding','Discuss fertility before treatment where relevant. Ask for a written contraception plan covering all four medicines and the period after treatment. Report possible pregnancy promptly and do not breastfeed unless your team confirms it is safe.'),item('Support and choices','Ask for an interpreter, accessible information, psychological support or a trusted person to join appointments. Discuss alternatives, expected benefits and lasting harms. You can revisit your preferences as treatment continues.')]},{'id':'after-treatment','title':'Care after a drug stops','intro':'Stopping chemotherapy or immunotherapy changes the plan but does not immediately remove all risks.','items':[item('After the second pemetrexed dose','Finish the prescribed 21-day folic-acid tail after the last pemetrexed. Review anti-sickness and rash-steroid stop dates. B12 is not automatically continued just because immune therapy continues.'),item('After the last immune dose',immune+' Keep the alert card and tell other healthcare professionals about both immune medicines. Ask who will arrange follow-up and review any unresolved symptoms.'),item('Use your team’s urgent instructions','When you call, state your treatment names, last treatment date, what changed, temperature if known, and whether you can drink and pass urine. Do not delay calling while gathering every detail.')] }]
